#version 450
#extension GL_GOOGLE_include_directive : require

/**
 * One bitonic compare-exchange stage over the whole key array (S3.3).
 *
 * ## Why the particles are sorted at all
 *
 * The same reason 1.4's blended surfaces are, and the reason the answer there does not
 * work here. Premultiplied `src + dst * (1 - src.a)` is order-dependent, so a plume of
 * smoke drawn in pool order composites wrongly wherever two puffs overlap -- and unlike
 * a scene of blended *objects*, there is no per-object granularity to sort at: the
 * objects are the particles, there are thousands of them, and they interpenetrate by
 * construction. That is exactly what the roadmap's S3.3 row says.
 *
 * ## Why bitonic
 *
 * It is the sort whose comparison network is fixed. Every pass touches the same pairs
 * whatever the data holds, so there is no scan, no scatter and no atomic anywhere in
 * it -- which means the result is bit-identical run to run, and the golden suite can
 * cover a particle scene. A radix sort would be fewer passes and would need a prefix
 * sum whose output ordering is not, in general, something this engine could promise.
 *
 * ## Why the domain is the whole pool
 *
 * A bitonic network sorts a power-of-two array, and the pool is one. Dead slots carry
 * PARTICLE_DEAD_KEY, which is the largest key there is, so they land after every live
 * particle and the draw simply takes the first `aliveCount` entries. Padding to a power
 * of two costs nothing here because the pool is already sized to the steady-state
 * population -- see ParticleSystem::requiredCapacity. Sorting a fixed 65536-slot pool
 * to order 500 particles is the cost this avoids, and it avoids it by not having a
 * fixed pool.
 *
 * ## Why there are two shaders
 *
 * Passes with `j >= BLOCK` compare elements in different workgroups and must go through
 * memory; this is that one. Passes with `j < BLOCK` stay inside a block and are done
 * entirely in shared memory by particle_sort_local.comp, which collapses nine
 * dispatches into one. At 4096 particles that is 15 dispatches instead of 78.
 */

#include "particle.glsl"

layout(local_size_x = 256) in;

layout(push_constant) uniform Push {
    uint k; ///< bitonic stage width; the sort direction of element i is (i & k) == 0
    uint j; ///< distance between the two elements a thread compares
    uint capacity;
    uint pad;
} pc;

void main() {
    uint i = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;
    if (i >= pc.capacity) return;

    uint partner = i ^ pc.j;
    // Each pair is handled by exactly one of its two threads, and the *other* one has
    // to return rather than do the same swap: two threads writing both elements of a
    // pair is a race whatever the values are.
    if (partner <= i) return;

    uint a = sortKeys[i];
    uint b = sortKeys[partner];
    bool ascending = (i & pc.k) == 0u;

    if ((a > b) == ascending) {
        sortKeys[i] = b;
        sortKeys[partner] = a;
    }
}
